    "finish_elapsed_s": 11.259757063991856,
    "text": "A refrigerator works like a heat-moving machine, not a cold-making one. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes, changing between liquid and gas. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas, which makes the inside cold. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, heating it up. Next, the hot gas flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases its heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it cycles back inside to soak up more heat. So the fridge doesn't create cold—it grabs heat from your food and dumps it outside!"
  }
